<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#FFFFFF" />
    <base href="/" />
    @* No static <title> here: every page supplies its own through <PageTitle>, and with
       prerendering the HeadOutlet emits a real <title> element into this head. *@
    <meta name="description" content="bit Bonboarding (Bit.Bonboarding) - a Blazor-native onboarding and product tour library: spotlights, contextual hints, branching and multi-page tours, with every decision made in C#." />

    @* Link previews: the card a chat client or a social site unfurls is the first impression a
       shared link makes. *@
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="Bit.Bonboarding" />
    <meta property="og:title" content="Bonboarding - onboarding and product tours for Blazor" />
    <meta property="og:description" content="Spotlights, branching and multi-page product tours for Blazor. Every decision made in C#; the browser only measures, observes and routes keys." />
    <meta property="og:image" content="https://bonboarding.bitplatform.dev/icon-512.png" />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary" />

    <ResourcePreloader />
    @* The site kit (app.css, theme.js, site.js - shared by every Utilities demo), then the library's
       stylesheet, then this demo's own sample styles. demo.css comes after the library on purpose:
       it restyles the tour card through the library's --bit-bonboarding-* custom properties, which
       only wins over the library's defaults later in the cascade - the order the theming page tells
       an application to use. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["app.css"]" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["_content/Bit.Bonboarding/bit-bonboarding.css"]" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["demo.css"]" />
    @* Scoped-CSS bundle: includes the client project's *.razor.css files as well. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["Bit.Bonboarding.Demo.Server.styles.css"]" />
    @* Synchronous on purpose: applies the light/dark theme before first paint. *@
    <script src="@Assets["theme.js"]"></script>
    <ImportMap />
    <link rel="icon" href="favicon.ico" sizes="32x32" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="favicon.svg" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" sizes="512x512" href="icon-512.png" />
    <HeadOutlet @rendermode="InteractiveAuto" />
</head>

@* data-enhance-nav="false": a link followed before the runtime has started - the Server circuit on a
   first visit, the WebAssembly runtime on later ones - is a plain page load rather than an enhanced
   one. Enhanced navigation fetches and merges the next page while the runtime is still starting, and
   the router the runtime then attaches can render the address it read before the merge - the URL
   says /getting-started and the page is the landing page. Once the app is interactive its router
   handles every link itself and this attribute is not consulted. *@
<body data-enhance-nav="false">
    @* InteractiveAuto: the first visit runs on the server while the WebAssembly runtime downloads,
       later ones in the browser - which is why both projects register the Bonboarding services. The
       pages are prerendered either way, so the first response carries the docs. *@
    <Routes @rendermode="InteractiveAuto" />
    @* The server half of Auto runs over a circuit, and this is what the reader sees while it
       reconnects. *@
    <ReconnectModal />
    <div id="blazor-error-ui" data-nosnippet>
        An unhandled error has occurred.
        <a href="." class="reload">Reload</a>
        <span class="dismiss">&#128473;</span>
    </div>
    <script src="@Assets["_framework/blazor.web.js"]"></script>
    <script src="@Assets["site.js"]"></script>
</body>

</html>
